---
/**
 * SiteMark — the "A", and the way home from anywhere.
 *
 * A constructed geometric A on a 16-unit grid, drawn in one stroke weight:
 * two diagonals mitered to a sharp apex, and a crossbar that does not touch
 * them. The detached bar is the whole idea. The rail down the left edge of
 * every page is a stack of free-floating 1px dashes, and the crossbar is one
 * more of them, parked inside the letter.
 *
 * The gap is 0.5 units, measured at the bar's top corner where the leg leans
 * furthest inboard, which is the tightest point. That is wide enough to read as
 * deliberate from about 24px up and narrow enough that when it does close — a
 * 16px favicon on a low-density screen — the mark degrades into an ordinary
 * solid A rather than into a smudge. A mark whose failure mode is "still a
 * letter" is the only kind worth shipping at favicon size.
 *
 * One weight throughout, 1.5 units, because `design.md` says the hairline scale
 * has two steps and no third. A lighter crossbar was drawn and rejected for
 * introducing one.
 *
 * `currentColor`, so it themes for free: the anchor sets the ink and the glyph
 * follows, in either theme and in either forced state. `public/favicon.svg` is
 * the same geometry with real colours pinned, because a favicon renders with no
 * inherited colour to pick up.
 */
---

{/*
  No `aria-current` on home. The rail's Home dash already carries it, and two
  "current page" announcements for one destination is noise, not redundancy.
*/}
<a class="mark press" href="/" aria-label="Home">
  <svg
    class="mark__glyph"
    viewBox="0 0 16 16"
    aria-hidden="true"
    focusable="false"
  >
    <path
      d="M2.2 14.1 L8 3.1 L13.8 14.1"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="1.5"
      stroke-linejoin="miter"
      stroke-linecap="butt"></path>
    <path
      d="M5.9 10.4 H10.1"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="1.5"
      stroke-linecap="butt"></path>
  </svg>
</a>

<style>
  /* Above the rail (40), below the mobile trigger (60) and everything modal:
     an open nav panel or palette should cover the mark, not fight it. */
  .mark {
    --mark-size: 24px;

    /* Optical, and the same correction the masthead makes with its -0.04em:
       line up the ink, not the box. The glyph carries its own left bearing —
       on the 16-unit grid its leftmost point is the outer corner of the left
       foot, 1.537 units in, or 9.6% of the size — so a box flush with the rail
       would put the letter 2px inboard of the dashes under it. */
    margin-left: calc(var(--mark-size) * -0.096);

    position: fixed;
    top: clamp(1.25rem, 3.5vh, 2rem);
    /* Below 900px the shell clears `--page-pad + 2.5rem` on the left for the
       vertical MENU label. Sitting inside that strip is what keeps the mark
       out of the content column: the page scrolls past to the right of it
       rather than underneath it, and the MENU label is at the vertical middle,
       so the two never meet. */
    left: var(--page-pad);
    z-index: 50;
    display: block;
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .mark {
      /* The rail's own left edge. Mark and dashes share a column. */
      left: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.75rem);
    }
  }

  .mark:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .mark__glyph {
    width: var(--mark-size);
    height: var(--mark-size);
  }

  /* 40x40 hit area without a 40px layout box, the same `::before` trick the
     rail dashes use — the glyph stays exactly where `left` puts it. */
  .mark::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: -8px;
    top: -8px;
    width: 40px;
    height: 40px;
  }
</style>
